WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
Good to have you on Planetary and Daily, episode 76.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.440
Today is June 2nd, 2026.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.420
Here's your science and health briefing.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.760
Smartphone cameras can now measure resting heart rate

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.180
passively across skin tones using facial video alone.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.680
Researchers demonstrated that short clips captured

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.500
during ordinary phone use can extract both heart rate

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.200
and resting heart rate without any attached sensor.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.120
The system performed consistently across

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:29.300
pigmentation levels, marking the first real world

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.800
validation of passive smartphone monitoring.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:35.500
The approach relies on subtle color changes in skin

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.980
detected by the front camera and processed through AI models.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.680
Next steps include scaling the method

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.560
to track daily variability in larger populations.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.480
Now, shifting to a very different area of research.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:52.720
A new commentary examines how passive monitoring technologies

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.780
might affect physician's ability to perform core assessments

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.440
without automated assistance.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.400
Authors draw parallels to aviation

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.160
where automation increased rather than reduce

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:04.640
the need for human oversight.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.840
The piece argues for deliberate training protocols

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.060
that preserve diagnostic vigilance

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.560
while integrating new tools.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:14.360
No specific trial data are presented.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.360
The focus remains on implementation safeguards.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.580
On a different note, data from nearly 4,000 participants

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:23.580
showed reductions in both impulsive

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.400
and planned aggressive behavior,

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.720
regardless of dose, duration, age, or sex.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.880
DHA enrichment in prefrontal cortex membranes

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.060
is proposed as one mechanism supporting improved impulse

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.000
control and emotion regulation.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.020
Authors state there is now sufficient evidence

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.420
to consider supplementation in community,

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.380
clinical, and justice settings.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.740
The effect appeared at both low and high intakes.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.360
The meta-analysis covered 29 trials.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.540
Next up, the device continuously samples

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.840
serosprevenal fluid and analyzes biomarkers

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.080
in real time for patients with brain injuries.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.820
Earlier detection allows interventions

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.040
before standard culture results return.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.840
The system was tested in clinical settings

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.940
where timing directly affects outcomes.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.760
Further validation across additional injury types is planned.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.300
Building on that mechanistic view,

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.740
we take a short deep dive into how memory retrieval

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:20.800
actually operates.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.780
Researchers mapped fine scale anatomy in the RSC

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.460
and linked specific cellular arrangements

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:30.460
to successful recall and spatial orientation.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.560
The work used rodent models to trace

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.000
how these circuits integrate past experience

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.080
with current position.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.800
Disruption of the identified structures

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.440
impaired both memory access and wayfinding.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.620
Follow-up studies will test whether

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.960
similar organization exists in humans.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.660
The Queensland Brain Institute work

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.840
isolates specific cellular structures

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.780
in the retro-splenial cortex

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.900
that enable memory retrieval during navigation.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.560
By linking these microstructures to behavioral performance

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:02.620
and rodents, the study clarifies

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:04.440
how distributed circuits convert

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.140
stored information into action.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.580
Most people picture memory as a mental video library

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.000
that simply replays stored scenes on demand.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.420
In reality, retrieval is an active reconstruction

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.200
that pulls elements from multiple brain regions

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.260
and reassembles them each time.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.220
Right now, as you recall what you had for breakfast,

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:27.400
your retro-splenial cortex is helping bind spatial context,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.180
time stamps, and sensory details into a coherent episode

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:33.520
rather than pulling a fixed file.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:37.080
Signals supporting this binding travel through circuits

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.260
that can be mapped at the level of individual cell clusters

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:41.700
not diffuse networks.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:44.760
Activities strengthening spatial navigation

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.540
such as varying your daily walking routes

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.740
may support the same circuits used for everyday recall.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.200
Watch for upcoming human studies

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.640
that test whether targeted navigation training

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.100
improves memory performance in older adults.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.180
Now, shifting focus.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:04.160
The attenuated systems allow simultaneous regulation

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.040
of multiple RNA targets without crosstalk.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.880
Researchers demonstrated programmable on-off switching

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.880
across several genes in a single cell.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.120
The approach expands the toolkit

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.060
for engineering microbial communities

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:18.880
with precise expression patterns.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:21.180
Further work will adapt the switches

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:22.900
to additional bacterial species.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:27.540
The new CAS 13D variants were reported in nature biotechnology.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:31.040
From engineered microbes, we look at engineered habitats

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.500
that aim to offset biodiversity loss.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.660
Created meadows in the Netherlands compensated

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.320
for habitat loss to development

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.800
and sustained bee and hoverfly populations.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:43.500
The study provides the first direct test

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.560
of biodiversity offsetting effectiveness for these insects.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:50.200
Species richness and abundance were comparable

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.380
to reference sites when floral resources were adequate.

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:57.280
Monitoring will continue to assess long-term stability.

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:00.840
The findings appeared in the Journal of Applied Ecology.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.660
Before we go, keep an eye on follow-up work

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:07.240
testing whether similar RSC organization exists in humans.

